### Difference Between 'throw' and 'throws' in Java

| Parameter | `throw` Keyword | `throws` Keyword |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Purpose** | Used to **explicitly instantiate and throw** an exception. | Used to **declare** that a method may throw exceptions. |
| **Location** | Written **inside** the method body. | Appended to the **method signature** header. |
| **Syntax** | Followed by an **instance** (`throw new Exception();`). | Followed by class **types** (`throws IOException, SQLException`). |
| **Multiplicity** | Can throw only **one** exception object at a time. | Can declare **multiple** comma-separated exception classes. |

```java
// 'throws' in method declaration
public static void checkEligibility(int age) throws IllegalArgumentException {
    if (age < 18) {
        // 'throw' inside method body
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Age must be >= 18");
    }
}
```

$$\therefore \text{'throw' acts (causes the error), while 'throws' declares (warns the caller).}$$
